{{also|Dervish}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|darwaysh|darwesh|dervice|dervis|dervise||archaic}}

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|ota|درویش|tr=derviş}}, from {{der|en|fa|درویش|tr=darvêš|t=poor; Sufi mystic}}, from {{der|en|pal|dlgwš|tr=driyōš|t=poor, needy}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈdɜːvɪʃ/|a=UK}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|Islam}} An [[itinerant]] [[Sufi]] [[mystic]] practicing [[voluntary]] [[poverty]] in [[devotion]] to [[God]], and often engaging in [[ecstatic]] [[ritual]]s.
#: {{syn|en|fakir}}
## {{lb|en|historical}} A citizen or inhabitant of [[Darawiish]] (''[[circa]]'' 1895–1920  {{C.E.}}), an anti-colonial polity in modern Somalia headed by Sufis.
## {{lb|en|historical}} One of the [[followers]] of {{w|Muhammad Ahmad}}, the [[Mahdi]] of the [[Sudan]], in the 1880s.
## {{lb|en|by extension}} Any irregular guerrilla fighter resembling the Sudanese Mahdi in equipment, uniform, tactics, etc.
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1926|author=w:T.E. Lawrence|title=Seven Pillars of Wisdom|location=New York|publisher=Anchor|year_published=1991|page=104
|passage=The [[w:Arab Revolt|Hejaz war]], meanwhile, would be one of '''dervishes''' against regular troops.}}

